# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 have "begin_needs_existing" and "begin_needs_existing → (once_nothing → nothing_now)"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 has "begin_needs_existing"; they differ when only "at one time there was nothing in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "at one time there was nothing in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true and "even now nothing would be in existence" is false. Is S5, "Now if this were true, even now there would be nothing in existence, because that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S9 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 have "¬regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 has "¬◇regress"; they differ when only "possibly, there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S9 is read aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 with no modality; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "possibly, there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another" is true and "there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another" is false. Is S9, "Now it is impossible to go on to infinity in necessary things which have their necessity caused by another, as has been already proved in regard to efficient causes.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 has "contingent_sometime_not → (all_contingent → once_nothing)" and "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_necessity_caused → regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_necessity_caused → regress"; they differ when only "that which is possible not to be at some time is not", "everything is possible not to be", "there exists something the existence of which is necessary", "every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another" and "there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another" are true, which the second allows; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 has "begin_needs_existing → (once_nothing → nothing_now)" and "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "necessary_exists ∧ all_necessity_caused → regress"
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 takes for granted "if that which is possible not to be at some time is not, then (if everything is possible not to be, then at one time there was nothing in existence)"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 do not
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if that which is possible not to be at some time is not, then (if everything is possible not to be, then at one time there was nothing in existence)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 does not
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 P3 (S3): the sentence says "impossible", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that which is possible not to be at some time is not"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 P9 (S11): the sentence has no word of "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, not receiving it from another": is it the sentence P9 came from?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 P3 (S3): the sentence says "impossible", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that which is possible not to be at some time is not"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 P9 (S11): the sentence has no word of "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, not receiving it from another": is it the sentence P9 came from?
